# License & Config
# ---------------

/site/config/.license

# NOTE: config.php is listed here but IS tracked — git keeps tracking
# files already in the index, ignore rule or not. The practical effect is
# that edits to it do not show up in `git status`, so they are easy to
# miss. Use `git status site/config/config.php` or `git diff HEAD --
# site/config/config.php` after changing it. It contains no secrets (the
# Kirby license key lives in .license, which is genuinely untracked).
/site/config/config.php

# Composer dependencies
# ---------------
#
# Both of these are installed by `composer install`, not committed.
# Kirby lands in kirby/ rather than vendor/ because getkirby/cms is
# type "kirby-cms" and getkirby/composer-installer puts it there.
# index.php does `require 'kirby/bootstrap.php'`, so the server needs
# composer install to have run. See "Updating the site" in README.md.

/vendor/
/kirby/

# Environment files
# ---------------

.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# PHP error logs
# ---------------

error_log
php_error.log

# Backup files
# ---------------

*.bak
*.backup
*.sql
*.sql.gz

# Content
# ---------------
#
# The site's actual content is NOT in version control. The production
# server is the source of truth; backups are rsync mirrors pulled nightly
# to content_backups/ by scripts/sync-blog.sh (see scripts/README.md).
#
# Why not git: Panel edits produced commits like "update(page): links"
# with no meaningful diff, no branching, and nothing anyone would ever
# revert. Git is for the layer between core Kirby and content.
#
# Consequence worth remembering: a fresh clone has NO content, so it will
# render a site with no pages. Pull content from the server or from
# content_backups/ to work on templates locally.
/content/

# Remote content backups (rsync target, see scripts/sync-blog.sh)
# ---------------
/content_backups/
/archive/

